*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !SOLO_STORE_PW(account, password, ok)
*
* A one-shot "sd <command>" - a scheduled job - proves the account password
* without being asked, from a record kept for it.  The installer
* (solo_password ACCOUNT) and SET.PASSWORD call this after !CRED_SET; login's
* require.password reads it.
*
* ***THE PASSWORD IS KEPT IN CLEAR.***  The record is $STORED in the $cred
* directory of the tree:  <1> the account name, upper-cased (the $cred key it
* belongs to);  <2> the password.  Nothing else on Linux can be unlocked by
* "the user" without a prompt: there is no per-user key store that a job
* running with no session can reach, so the protection is the file's
* permissions and its directory's - $cred is created mode 0700 by the stager,
* the tree is the user's own, and sd runs with a umask that makes every file
* it creates private to the user (0600).  Anyone who can read the user's files
* can read this password, exactly as they could read a ~/.pgpass.  That is
* the owner's ruling, and the docs must say it in these words.
*
* The '$' key keeps it out of cred_set's partner search and out of any
* account's name (an account name starts with a letter, solo_account).
*
* Read back through a fresh open, and compared, before ok is set - a record
* written but unusable would only show at the next scheduled job.
*
* The password is never displayed or logged.  ok false: status() says why.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine SOLO_STORE_PW(account, password, ok)
$catalog !SOLO_STORE_PW

$include keys.h
$include err.h

   ok = @false
   set.status 0

   acc = upcase(trim(account))
   if acc = '' or acc[1,1] = '$' or password = '' then
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to cred.f else
      set.status ER$NVR
      return
   end

   rec = ''
   rec<1> = acc
   rec<2> = password
   write.ok = @true
   write rec to cred.f, '$STORED' on error
      write.ok = @false
   end
   close cred.f
   rec = ''
   if not(write.ok) then
      set.status ER$PERM
      return
   end

*  Read back through a fresh open: the check is that login could use it, not
*  that the bytes landed.  compare(), not "=": "=" compares two numeric
*  strings as numbers, so it would call "007" and "7" the same password.
   check = ''
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to chk.f then
      read check from chk.f, '$STORED' else check = ''
      close chk.f
   end
   same = (check<1> = acc and compare(check<2>, password) = 0)
   check = ''
   if not(same) then
      set.status ER$WRITE.ERROR
      return
   end

   ok = @true
   return
end

* END-CODE
